Subject: [PATCH] staging: t2bce: rebuild the BCE transport across hibernation

The image-loading kernel probes t2bce (the LUKS prompt needs the internal
keyboard) and signs on to bridgeOS with SET_FW_PROTOCOL_VERSION (0x0c).
Decoded bridgeOS 23P6068 firmware: a sign-on destroys every queue of the
host owner, and the saved-state nonce is written only by the serializer, so
RESTORE_STATE_AND_WAKE after a Linux loader takes the equal-nonce fast path
and deserializes nothing. The restored kernel then drove queue ids and DMA
addresses bridgeOS no longer had, while the loader's queues could still DMA
into restored memory (iommu=pt). No saved state can be carried across S4.

core: writer freeze/thaw quiesce without a firmware sleep handshake; poweroff
keeps the stateful suspend path. PM_RESTORE_PREPARE identifies the image-loading
kernel, whose freeze signs on to drop all host queues before memory handoff.
If loading aborts, thaw rebuilds the command queues. The restored kernel signs
on again, resets the command CQ/SQ rings and re-registers them.
vhci: PM notifier. PM_HIBERNATION_PREPARE / PM_RESTORE_PREPARE remove the
HCD (all transfer queues) and destroy event/message queues while firmware
and usbcore are running; PM_POST_* rebuild them with a fresh HCD.
audio: same notifier, but the card stays registered: streams are suspended,
remote access is turned off and the BCE queues are released under a
transport rwsem that excludes senders. Afterwards the queues are rebuilt,
the alive handshake is repeated, and the device list, UIDs and BufferStruct
layout are checked against probe before the card is reused; on any mismatch
the device is unbound and probed again. Userspace never sees the card leave,
so WirePlumber cannot race a removal against the re-add (0.5.17 lost the
speaker node that way). remove() follows the hot-unplug model and destroys
its BCE queues, which were leaked with receive buffers posted.

Queue teardown:
- dma: the completion IRQ is synchronized before an SQ is freed; the
  dispatcher could still hold a pointer to it.
- A failed unregister keeps the ring and qid in quarantine and returns
  -EIO. Submission stops until a sign-on revokes every host queue and
  releases the quarantine (t2bce_core_revoke_firmware_queues()).
- Writer freeze refuses while queues are quarantined.
- VHCI unregisters transfer SQs before giving URBs back, and revokes on
  failure. Clients free posted buffers only after a successful unregister
  or a revoke.
- audio: the playback hrtimer queued snd_pcm_period_elapsed() from hard
  IRQ on a nonatomic PCM (a mutex): "scheduling while atomic", then a
  lockup with any period-wakeup client. The timer now counts periods and a
  work item delivers them. trigger() handles SUSPEND.

S3 callbacks are unchanged apart from the SUSPEND trigger. Tested on
7.2.6-arch2-Watanare-T2-2-t2: 5/5 real S4 restores with core 07CBD5DD, one
with loader-side queue teardown (core 9F39176E), and one with this revision
(dma 1DC122D0, core E9020F90, VHCI 3499BD86, audio 331CAD19): same boot ID,
card kept, PipeWire speaker sink present, sound and input confirmed. The
unregister-failure paths are covered by source-level fault injection only.
See docs/HIBERNATION.md for evidence and remaining limitations.

